--TEST--
`Core\Cache::memory()` names this process's one store rather than handing back a container — a handle taken before every write reads them all, either handle reads what the other wrote, and an entry outlives every handle that wrote it — `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers` and `rule:concurrency/the-memory-tier-is-one-store-per-process`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The tier is *one store per process*, so the value `memory()` answers is a
// name for it and not the thing holding the entries. A store that were the
// object would still pass every case written through a single handle: what it
// fails is a second handle, which is the shape a real program has — one taken
// in a request's entry point and another taken by whatever it calls.
var $first = Core\Cache::memory();

// Taken before a single entry exists, so nothing it reads below can be a
// snapshot it was handed at this line.
var $second = Core\Cache::memory();

array<string> $keys = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma", "delta"];
$first->put("alpha", "<alpha>");
$first->put("beta", "<beta>");
$second->put("gamma", "<gamma>");
$second->put("delta", "<delta>");

// A third handle, taken after all four writes, reads every one of them —
// counted rather than read off a line, because a store that were per-handle
// would answer for the two its own handle wrote and `null` for the others,
// which is a plausible-looking half.
var $third = Core\Cache::memory();
tainted string $back = "";
int $seen = 0;
foreach ($keys as string $key) {
    $back = $third->get($key) as string;
    if ($back == "<" . $key . ">") {
        $seen = $seen + 1;
    }
}
echo "a handle taken last reads them all: ", $seen, " of ", Core\Arr::count($keys), "\n";

// The same claim in the direction time runs against: the handle taken first
// reads what the handle taken second wrote afterwards.
tainted string $crossed = $first->get("delta") as string;
echo "the first handle reads the second's write: ", $crossed, "\n";

// And the entries are the process's rather than any handle's: rebinding all
// three variables drops every handle that wrote any of this, and what they
// wrote is still there to be read through one taken after the fact.
$first = Core\Cache::memory();
$second = Core\Cache::memory();
$third = Core\Cache::memory();
var $fourth = Core\Cache::memory();
tainted string $survived = $fourth->get("alpha") as string;
echo "an entry outlives every handle that wrote it: ", $survived, "\n";
--EXPECT--
a handle taken last reads them all: 4 of 4
the first handle reads the second's write: <delta>
an entry outlives every handle that wrote it: <alpha>
